---
// firma2-map-panel — a panel that floats ON a map rather than beside it: pinned
// to one inline edge of a positioned surface, as tall as its content up to the
// height of that surface, scrolling inside itself, and able to be taken off the
// screen and put back without moving anything else.
//
// WHAT IT IS FOR. A map-first screen has one surface and everything else is laid
// over it. firma2-project-atlas floats three of these — the project's record on
// the leading edge, its tracked sections on the trailing edge, and the control
// that clears both — and the only thing that differs between them is which edge
// they take, how wide they are, and whether they draw a surface of their own.
// That is a component, not three copies of the same twelve declarations.
//
// IT IS NOT A RAIL, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DISTINCTION. firma2-record-rail is the
// docked version of this idea and stays the right answer on a page whose main
// column is a document: it is `position: sticky` inside a real layout track, it
// takes the content pane's own background, and it is ruled off by a hairline
// because it sits BESIDE the content. A panel here sits OVER the content, so it
// needs the opposite of all four — it is out of flow, it draws a floating
// surface, it carries an edge and a shadow, and it covers what is under it.
// Both exist because both readings of "put the record next to the map" are
// legitimate; which one a page wants is the page's decision.
//
// COVERING THE MAP IS A REAL COST, and the surface underneath has to be told.
// firma2-project-map exposes --firma2-map-plate-inset-inline-start for its own
// chrome and takes a fit reservation through its reflow event; the atlas pushes
// both past whatever this panel is occupying. That negotiation was tried once
// before on the project detail page and abandoned (see firma2-record-rail's
// header) — the difference is that there it bought nothing, and here the whole
// screen is the map, so there is no version of this layout that avoids it.
//
// THE COLLAPSED STATE IS AN ATTRIBUTE, NOT A PROP. `data-collapsed` on the host
// is the API: a parent's script sets and clears it, and this file owns what that
// looks like. It is deliberately not a server prop — the state changes on a
// click, long after the server rendered anything — and deliberately not a rule
// in the parent's stylesheet, which would mean the parent reaching through a
// component boundary to style a box it does not own. It is also why the panel
// leaves with `visibility`, not `display`: hidden it is out of the tab order and
// out of the accessibility tree, which is the whole of what `display: none` was
// wanted for, and it can still be transitioned back.
//
// bcn-lego-checked: walked the catalog (`ls
// node_modules/@esa/ecology/src/components/`) for a persistent floating surface
// and there is none. esa-popover and esa-tooltip are anchored TRANSIENTS — they
// open against a trigger and close again, and neither is a region that holds a
// page's content for as long as the page lasts. esa-side-dialog is a modal
// drawer: it takes focus, lays a scrim over the app and is dismissed, which is
// the opposite of a panel the reader works alongside. esa-card is what goes
// INSIDE this (the atlas slots four of them into the trailing panel) and is a
// box in a column, with a header band and a padding step sized for a section of
// a document — firma2-project-map's own header already argues that case for the
// small plates it draws, and this is that argument at panel scale. esa-container
// is a max-width content wrapper, not a surface. Beacon is not cloned on this
// machine. The layout inside is `.stack`, the hub primitive; no flex is
// hand-rolled here, and every value below is a token. This file is the reusable
// home for the pattern — a second map screen composes it rather than restating
// the surface, the scroll behaviour and the collapse.
import type { HTMLAttributes } from 'astro/types';

interface Props extends HTMLAttributes<'div'> {
  /**
   * The panel's accessible name. Present makes this a COMPLEMENTARY LANDMARK —
   * an <aside> a screen-reader user can jump to and skip past by name, which is
   * what a panel holding a section of the record needs to be.
   *
   * Omit for a panel that holds only chrome (a toolbar, one button). A landmark
   * per control makes the landmark list longer without making it more useful,
   * and the control already names itself.
   */
  label?: string;
  /** Which inline edge the panel is pinned to. */
  side: 'start' | 'end';
  /**
   * `plate` (default) draws the floating surface — fill, hairline, radius,
   * shadow — for content that has none of its own.
   *
   * `bare` draws nothing and lets the slotted content be the surface. The
   * atlas's trailing panel is a column of esa-cards, each already a white box
   * with an edge; a plate behind them would be the boxes-inside-boxes read that
   * firma2-rail-section deleted a border to avoid.
   */
  surface?: 'plate' | 'bare';
  /**
   * The panel's width, when this panel's width is its OWN business — `auto` for
   * a panel holding a single control, which is as wide as that control.
   *
   * Omit it and the panel reads --firma2-map-panel-width off its surface,
   * falling back to the 22rem the record's cards were written for. That is the
   * form a composing surface wants: the width is the same fact as the map's
   * reservation for it, so both have to come from one declaration, and only the
   * surface can make it. Passed here it becomes an inline style and no ancestor
   * can reach it — which is correct for the toolbar and wrong for everything a
   * layout has to reason about.
   */
  width?: string;
  /** Gap between slotted children, on the `.stack` scale. */
  gap?: '2xs' | 'xs' | 'sm' | 'md' | 'lg' | 'xl';
}

const { label, side, surface = 'plate', width, gap = 'xl', class: className, ...rest } = Astro.props;

const Tag = label ? 'aside' : 'div';
---
{/* An <aside> ONLY when it is named — see the `label` prop. An unnamed
    complementary landmark is worse than no landmark: it appears in the list a
    screen-reader user navigates by and tells them nothing about whether it is
    the one they want. */}
<Tag
  class:list={['firma2-map-panel', className]}
  data-side={side}
  data-surface={surface}
  aria-label={label}
  style={width ? `--firma2-map-panel-width: ${width}` : undefined}
  {...rest}
>
  {/* THE HOST IS THE SCROLL CONTAINER AND THE STACK IS INSIDE IT — the same
      split firma2-record-rail makes, and it is load-bearing for a reason that
      cost this component a round of debugging.

      The scroller was the stack itself for a version, with the host a flex
      parent it filled. That is one element fewer and it silently CRUSHED every
      card in it: a flex column with a definite height shrinks its children to
      fit before it ever considers scrolling, so four cards needing 1100px inside
      a 720px panel each came out a fraction of their height with their content
      clipped — a funding table showing its header row and nothing else. A block
      scroll container has no such behaviour; its children keep their heights and
      the overflow is what scrolls, which is the whole point of a scroll
      container. `overflow-y` on a box with a radius also clips to that radius,
      so the surface below needs no separate `overflow: hidden`.

      overscroll-behavior so a wheel that bottoms out in here does not chain into
      the page beneath. On a screen whose background is a MAP that is not a
      nicety: a scroll escaping the panel would pan or zoom the map, and the
      reader did not ask to go anywhere. */}
  <div class="firma2-map-panel__inner stack" data-gap={gap}>
    <slot />
  </div>
</Tag>

<style>
  .firma2-map-panel {
    /* THE ONE STEP EVERY EDGE USES. The panel sits --spacing-400 off the
       surface's edges on both axes, which is the same inset firma2-project-map
       gives its own plates — so a panel and a plate on one map line up rather
       than each keeping its own margin. The block-start is a knob because a
       surface can carry more than one panel on an edge and the second has to
       start below the first; the atlas stacks its toolbar and its sections that
       way. */
    --firma2-map-panel-inset: var(--spacing-400, 1rem);
    --firma2-map-panel-inset-block-start: var(--firma2-map-panel-inset);

    position: absolute;
    /* Above firma2-project-map's own plates, which claim z-index 1 inside the
       stacking context that section opens. A panel is the thing a reader came
       to read; a plate is the map's tooling, and if they ever overlap the
       reading wins. */
    z-index: 2;

    inset-block-start: var(--firma2-map-panel-inset-block-start);
    /* AS TALL AS ITS CONTENT, UP TO THE SURFACE. Not `inset-block: 0` — that
       stretches a short panel to the full height and leaves it mostly empty,
       covering map for no reason. The max is what is left of the surface after
       both insets, so the panel stops clear of the bottom edge and starts
       scrolling instead of running off it. */
    max-block-size: calc(
      100% - var(--firma2-map-panel-inset-block-start) - var(--firma2-map-panel-inset)
    );

    inline-size: var(--firma2-map-panel-width, 22rem);
    /* A NARROW WINDOW MUST NOT PUT THE PANEL OFF THE SURFACE. Two panels at
       22rem need 44rem plus three gutters before they even meet in the middle,
       and the app's nav rail is outside that. This is the floor that keeps each
       one on screen; the atlas takes the pair off the map entirely below the
       width where that stops being readable. */
    max-inline-size: calc(100% - var(--firma2-map-panel-inset) * 2);

    overflow-y: auto;
    overscroll-behavior: contain;

    /* THE PANEL LEAVES AND COMES BACK, and this is the whole of that behaviour.
       `visibility` rather than `display` so it can be transitioned, and because
       hidden visibility already does the two things that matter — out of the tab
       order, out of the accessibility tree. The delay is what keeps it on screen
       long enough to fade: it flips at the END of the exit and at the START of
       the return, which is what the `0s` in the collapsed rule below is doing.

       --transition-base is the tier-2 role for exactly this ("a change big
       enough to want to be watched — a panel resizing, a floating button
       arriving"), and naming the role rather than a duration is also what
       answers prefers-reduced-motion: @esa/tokens re-points the transition
       tokens to --duration-0 inside its own reduced-motion block, so there is no
       media query to write here. */
    transition:
      opacity var(--transition-base, 200ms ease),
      translate var(--transition-base, 200ms ease),
      visibility 0s;
  }

  .firma2-map-panel[data-side='start'] {
    inset-inline-start: var(--firma2-map-panel-inset);
  }

  .firma2-map-panel[data-side='end'] {
    inset-inline-end: var(--firma2-map-panel-inset);
  }

  /* THE FLOATING SURFACE. `elevation-floating` and `elevation-2` are the pair
     the system already uses for a surface that sits OVER content rather than in
     it — the same two firma2-project-map's plates and its shape tooltips
     resolve, which is what keeps a plate, a tooltip and a panel on one map
     looking like the same product.

     The radius clips the scrolled content on its own — a box with `overflow-y:
     auto` is a clip box — so there is no `overflow: hidden` here. */
  .firma2-map-panel[data-surface='plate'] {
    background: var(--color-background-elevation-floating, #fcfcfc);
    border: var(--border-width-default, 1px) solid var(--color-border-default-subtle, #d9d9d9);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md, 0.5rem);
    box-shadow: var(--elevation-2, 0 2px 12px 0 rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.04));
    padding: var(--spacing-400, 1rem);
  }

  /* NO PADDING on the bare variant: its children carry their own surface and
     their own padding, and a step here would inset a column of cards from an
     edge that is not drawn. */

  /* GONE, AND THE DIRECTION IT LEAVES IN IS THE EDGE IT CAME FROM. A panel that
     faded straight out could have been anywhere; sliding a step toward its own
     edge says where it went and where the click will bring it back from.
     A physical translate rather than a logical one: `translate` takes x before
     y and has no logical form, so the leading edge is the left one. This spoke
     is LTR throughout; an RTL build would want these two swapped. */
  .firma2-map-panel[data-collapsed] {
    opacity: 0;
    visibility: hidden;
    transition:
      opacity var(--transition-base, 200ms ease),
      translate var(--transition-base, 200ms ease),
      /* Duration 0s, DELAY --transition-base: the shorthand reads the first time
         as the duration and the second as the delay, so this is "flip it at the
         end of the fade". Naming the same role as the delay is what keeps the
         two in step — including at --duration-0 under reduced motion, where the
         fade is instant and a hardcoded 200ms delay would leave an invisible
         panel swallowing clicks for a fifth of a second. */
      visibility 0s var(--transition-base, 200ms ease);
  }

  .firma2-map-panel[data-collapsed][data-side='start'] {
    translate: calc(var(--spacing-400, 1rem) * -1) 0;
  }

  .firma2-map-panel[data-collapsed][data-side='end'] {
    translate: var(--spacing-400, 1rem) 0;
  }
</style>
